he `/home` directory and databases into cloud storage are also available, as Azure VM Restore Point will only include the OS disk. All database backups were verified against the cloud storage.\n\n\nAll identified database backups have been successfully verified against cloud storage locations, with size and cryptographic hash matching expected values. These validations provide confidence in the integrity and recoverability of the data backups. The process will move on to confirming restore procedures.\n\n\nVerified the integrity of the GCP Redis database backup, the database manifest, and the Azure home projects backup through cryptographic hash and size validation against cloud storage. The cloud storage security boundary restricts object deletion and overwriting, with read access currently enabled via default ACLs which I intend to further restrict. I'll exclude virtual kernel filesystems from the coverage calculation as they are not persistent.\n\n\nAzure Docker containers' persistent data is wholly captured within the project archive and database backups, validating their recoverability.  GCP's persistent disk and Azure's OS disk are fully covered by their respective backups and database backups.  Azure's `/home` projects and configs are secured through the cloud storage vault archive.\n\nStructural integrity of various databases (SQLite, PostgreSQL, MariaDB, CockroachDB) was confirmed through integrity checks and valid syntax. The GCP machine image, database dumps, and project archive's restore capabilities are all verified. A complete end-to-end VM spin-up and database import was not executed.\n\n\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
